  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The ten-item cutoff lacks justification from training-history lengths or timestamp gaps. Report the fraction of supervised and validation histories actually affected; ten interactions do not represent a fixed elapsed-time horizon.",
    "Encoding every supervised position as a separate baseline-length window can multiply backbone computation substantially. Complete-example chunking may still leave too many windows in memory; specify window microbatching, loss normalization and a measured runtime feasibility check within the shared budget.",
    "Right-aligning every window can change positional features and dropout realizations. A full-context windowed control should first reproduce baseline evaluation scores where applicable; otherwise the experiment changes more than available context.",
    "Improvement from bounded-context retraining would support that configuration, but would not identify stale-history harm: regularization, positional changes and optimization effects remain alternative explanations. The report also overstates the shortcut argument; an identity shortcut carries its input state, whose historical content depends on preceding blocks.",
    "Originality 2 is reasonable and potential 6 is plausible but speculative. Difficulty 4 may underestimate overlapping-window computation, gradient accumulation and predictor consistency. Exact inherited settings, chunk sizes, checkpoint rules and reproducible qualitative-assessment criteria remain unspecified."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Resolve the main implementation and compute gap without changing the required NextItNet backbone or dropping supervised targets.",
      "query": "For causal dilated residual convolution networks such as NextItNet, how can every next-item training position be encoded from an independent raw-input suffix of at most ten items using shared parameters? Investigate window microbatching with exact position-mean gradient accumulation, padding and positional equivalence, dropout effects, and computational complexity compared with ordinary sequence training."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether a fixed ten-interaction horizon is a useful first control and clarify what a gain would establish.",
      "query": "What controlled studies of sequential recommendation compare retrained short-history and full-history models, particularly NextItNet on Amazon datasets? Look for history-length and elapsed-time stratification, persistent-interest tradeoffs, and controls distinguishing useful recency restriction from regularization or optimization effects."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Check the architectural rationale and calibrate novelty and difficulty against direct precedents.",
      "query": "In NextItNet and causal residual convolution networks, does gating only residual updates necessarily preserve influence from distant historical inputs? Analyze dependency propagation through identity shortcuts and preceding blocks, and compare strict raw-input sliding-window bounds with dilated convolution receptive fields and learned residual gates."
